course: dictionary
lesson: 10541
title: Ultramarine and the colours of words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a colour, and some name a thing that has that colour. Ultramarine is a bright, deep blue. «Ultramarine blue, made from a stone, once cost more than gold.» The sentence tells you the word is about a blue, and that the blue came from a special stone.
explain: Ultramarine can be a noun or an adjective. As a noun it names the blue paint powder. As an adjective it describes a thing: an ultramarine sky is a deep blue sky. Check by swapping in your meaning: if the sea looks like a deep blue, the word is describing the sea.
explain: Context also gives clues about value. Painters saved this blue for very special things, so the word is linked with cost and care, not just with colour. Ask what the sentence is about before you decide what the word means.
fact: Ultramarine means a bright, deep blue colour.
fact: As a noun, ultramarine can also mean the blue paint powder itself.
fact: As an adjective, ultramarine describes something that has this deep blue colour.
fact: Real ultramarine was made by grinding the blue stone lapis lazuli into a powder.
fact: The name means «beyond the sea», because the stone came from far away across the sea.
exercise: «Ultramarine blue, made from a stone, once cost more than gold.» What is ultramarine in this sentence? | A pale yellow paint | A kind of gold | A green stone from the hills | *A deep blue colour
exercise: Ms Rivera says, «The ultramarine sky was clear all day.» What is ultramarine doing in this sentence? | It is a kind of bird | It means cloudy or grey | *It describes the sky as a deep blue | It is the name of a town
exercise: Why were painters saving ultramarine for special things, such as a robe? | It was the only paint that dried quickly | It was too pale to see | It was the cheapest paint in the box | *It was very costly, more than gold
test: What does the name ultramarine mean? | *Beyond the sea | Blue sky | Gold stone | Ground powder
test: What was ultramarine paint made from? | Gold coins | Glass windows | Church bells | *Ground lapis lazuli, a blue stone
exam: Ultramarine can be a noun or an adjective; which sentence uses it as an adjective? | Ultramarine is her favourite word | *The ultramarine sea sparkled under the sun | She bought a tube of ultramarine | She mixed some ultramarine on her palette
